{"skill": "Inferences", "difficulty": "Medium", "question": "In this excerpt from Charles Dickens’s "Great Expectations," the narrator, Pip, reflects on the stark difference he feels between his rural upbringing and his new urban life:

"I never could have believed it without experience; but as Joe and Biddy became more at their cheerful home by the marshes, I became more at my desolate one, and I would fain not have gone back there."

Which statement is best supported by the excerpt?", "choices": ["A) Pip believes the marshes are less appealing than the city", "B) Pip cannot recall his experiences in the marshes", "C) Pip strongly wishes to return to the marshes at once", "D) Pip feels uncomfortable with the distance that now separates him from his former life"], "answer": "D", "explanation": "To make an inference, consider how Pip contrasts his life in the city with Joe and Biddy’s "cheerful home by the marshes." The phrase "I would fain not have gone back there" suggests a conflicted feeling: while Pip misses the warmth of his old home, he no longer feels at ease. Therefore, the best-supported inference is that Pip is troubled by the separation from his former life."}

