{"skill": "Inferences", "difficulty": "Medium", "question": "Charles Dickens’s novel Hard Times was first published in 1854. The following excerpt is spoken by Mr. Gradgrind, a character in the novel:

“Now, what I want is, Facts. Teach these boys and girls nothing but Facts. Facts alone are wanted in life. You can only form the minds of reasoning animals upon Facts: nothing else will ever be of any service to them.”

Which inference about Mr. Gradgrind’s viewpoint is best supported by the excerpt?", "choices": ["A) He believes that students should balance factual knowledge with imagination.", "B) He thinks that the emotional well-being of students should come before learning facts.", "C) He views creativity as an asset when it serves practical ends.", "D) He insists that factual information is the only meaningful basis for education."], "answer": "D", "explanation": "From the excerpt, Mr. Gradgrind clearly stresses the importance of facts above all else. He argues that they are the sole foundation of a useful education, implying that there is little to no place for imagination or emotion in his teaching philosophy. Therefore, the best inference is that he views factual information as the only significant way to shape a student’s mind."}

