{"skill": "Inferences", "difficulty": "Hard", "question": "The following excerpt is adapted from Fyodor Dostoevsky’s public-domain novel, "Crime and Punishment." In this scene, the character Raskolnikov is walking among a crowd after a tense and disorienting moment:

"He was so immersed in his own feelings that he scarcely saw or heard anything around him. This solitude in the midst of the throng still further excited him; he walked on like one pursued, unwillingly drawn into the bustle, yet incapable of escaping it altogether."

Which inference about Raskolnikov is best supported by the passage?", "choices": ["A) He feels heightened anxiety because his internal turmoil prevents him from attaining peace.", "B) He intentionally seeks crowded areas to assert his intellectual superiority over others.", "C) He is unfeeling and unable to register the presence of people around him.", "D) He finds deep relief in the busy environment, as it fully distracts him from his troubles."], "answer": "A", "explanation": "The excerpt describes Raskolnikov’s sense of discomfort and agitation in the crowd, implying that his inner worries and fears are magnified rather than soothed by the commotion. Answer A is correct because it infers that his deeper struggles create anxiety he cannot shake. The other choices are not well supported by the text, which suggests he is neither seeking to dominate others, fully indifferent, nor relieved by the crowd’s presence."}

